net: ethernet: mtk_ppe_offload: reject tc flowtable when HNAT owns the PPE

When the SDK HNAT module is part of the build (CONFIG_NET_MEDIATEK_HNAT /
CONFIG_NET_MEDIATEK_HNAT_MODULE), mtk_probe() compiles out PPE init and
mtk_eth_offload_init(), leaving eth->flow_table as an uninitialized
rhashtable (tbl == NULL). mtk_eth_setup_tc_block() still registers the
tc block callback, so a firewall flowtable with hardware offload enabled
calls into mtk_flow_offload_replace() -> rhashtable_lookup(), which
dereferences the NULL bucket table ("Unable to handle kernel read from
unreadable memory at virtual address 0000000000000008" inside
mtk_flow_offload_replace, workqueue nf_ft_offload_add) and panics the
board roughly when the WAN comes up.

Guard the callback with a runtime check: without a bucket table the
tc/PPE offload path cannot work; hardware NAT is handled by the HNAT
module through its own netfilter hooks instead.

Signed-off-by: local
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe_offload.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe_offload.c
@@ -1001,10 +1001,20 @@
 	flow_setup_cb_t *cb;

 	if (!eth->soc->offload_version)
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;

+	/* PPE flowtable is not initialized when the SDK HNAT module is
+	 * built: mtk_probe() compiles out PPE init and mtk_eth_offload_init()
+	 * for CONFIG_NET_MEDIATEK_HNAT(_MODULE), leaving eth->flow_table as
+	 * an uninitialized rhashtable (tbl == NULL). Serving a tc flowtable
+	 * offload request would dereference the NULL bucket table inside
+	 * rhashtable_lookup() and crash the kernel. Hardware NAT is handled
+	 * by the HNAT module through its own netfilter hooks instead. */
+	if (!eth->flow_table.tbl)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
 	if (f->binder_type != FLOW_BLOCK_BINDER_TYPE_CLSACT_INGRESS)
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;

 	cb = mtk_eth_setup_tc_block_cb;
 	f->driver_block_list = &block_cb_list;
